# Ignore-file for `Dockerfile`, whose build context is the PARENT of this repo.
#
# BuildKit reads `<dockerfile-name>.dockerignore` from beside the Dockerfile and
# gives it precedence over any `.dockerignore` at the context root. That is the
# only reason this file can live inside the Tovu repo and be committed with it,
# rather than being an uncommittable file in the developer's home directory.
# See: https://docs.docker.com/build/concepts/context/
#
# Strategy: exclude the entire context, then re-include exactly the two trees
# the build needs, then prune inside them. The parent directory holds every
# other project on the machine — tens of gigabytes that would otherwise be
# tarred up and sent to the daemon on every build.

# Everything, by default.
*

# The only two trees the build may see.
!Tovu
!Jini

# --- Prune inside Tovu -------------------------------------------------------

# Host-built dependencies. These carry macOS/arm64 binaries for better-sqlite3,
# argon2 and sharp; shipping them into a linux image produces the classic
# "builds fine, dies on require" failure. The build stages install fresh.
Tovu/node_modules
Tovu/apps/*/node_modules

# Build output — regenerated by `npm run build` and the two vite builds.
Tovu/dist
Tovu/apps/*/dist
Tovu/apps/*/dist-debug

# RUNTIME STATE, NEVER IMAGE CONTENT. Holds the developer's real content.db,
# its WAL sidecars, uploads/ and ops state. Baking it into a published image
# would ship their data to anyone who pulls it. The Dockerfile also `rm -rf`s
# this in the build stage so an edit here cannot quietly reintroduce it.
Tovu/infra

Tovu/.git
Tovu/.github
Tovu/coverage
Tovu/development/coverage
Tovu/**/*.log

# Agent-framework and working docs. Large, and nothing in the image reads them.
Tovu/AI-Dev-Shop
Tovu/ADS-memory
Tovu/foundry

# Test artifacts.
Tovu/test-results
Tovu/playwright-report
Tovu/development/e2e
Tovu/**/__tests__
Tovu/**/*.test.ts
Tovu/**/*.test.tsx

# Local env files. Secrets are passed at runtime, never baked in.
Tovu/.env
Tovu/.env.*
!Tovu/.env.example

# --- Prune inside Jini -------------------------------------------------------

# Same reasoning as Tovu's: stage 1 runs `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` to
# get linux binaries and a graph that matches the lockfile.
Jini/node_modules
Jini/**/node_modules

# Regenerated by `pnpm -r build`. Jini's dist/ is gitignored upstream, so a
# fresh clone has none either — excluding these keeps the context honest about
# what the build actually produces rather than silently reusing host output.
Jini/packages/*/dist
Jini/apps/*/dist

Jini/.git
Jini/.github
Jini/**/*.log
Jini/**/coverage
Jini/**/test-results
